WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.540
Least privilege means each database credential has only the access needed for its approved purpose.

2
00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:16.440
A runtime application account, read-only reporting account, migration account, administrative account, background worker account, and data pipeline account may need different privileges.

3
00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:24.780
Combining all of those functions into one broad administrator credential may be convenient, but it makes every exposure more damaging and every audit trail less useful.

4
00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:28.340
Runtime access should be separated from maintenance access.

5
00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:39.400
A web service that reads and writes application records usually should not have schema ownership, broad administrative capabilities, or unrestricted access to unrelated databases.

6
00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:48.140
Migration credentials may need schema-change privileges, but they should be limited to migration workflows and protected with strong operational controls.

7
00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:53.200
Reporting credentials may be read-only and restricted to approved views or datasets.

8
00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:58.200
Tenant, customer, and data-domain boundaries should influence credential design.

9
00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:05.100
In some systems, application logic enforces tenant isolation while the database account has broad table access.

10
00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:11.520
In other systems, separate schemas, databases, views, or accounts can reduce blast radius.

11
00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:18.980
The right model depends on architecture, but the principle is consistent: credentials should not grant access that the workload has no business using.

12
00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:24.940
Each credential should have an owner, a consumer, a purpose, and a review cadence.

13
00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:33.840
Service accounts and workload identity concepts can help tie database access to a specific application or job rather than a person or shared password.

14
00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:41.220
Avoiding broad administrator credentials in application runtimes makes rotation, incident response, and monitoring easier.

15
00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:46.600
When privilege is narrow, a mistake is less likely to become a system-wide data event.
